# Application secrets for LOCAL development, in dotenv syntax.
#
#   cp .dev.vars.example .dev.vars
#
# Wrangler loads this file into the Worker's env for `pnpm dev` and `pnpm preview`,
# so every key here is readable server-side as `platform.env.NAME` or through
# `$env/dynamic/private`. Nothing here reaches the browser.
#
# Keeping this file present also isolates .env: when .dev.vars exists, Wrangler
# excludes .env from the Worker env, so your CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN stays a CLI
# credential instead of becoming an app-visible variable.
#
# The project has no application secrets yet — this file documents the convention
# and gives the first one somewhere to go. Delete the sample line when you add a real key.

# EXAMPLE_API_KEY="local-development-value"

# Rules for anything you add here:
#   - Read it only from src/lib/server/** , +page.server.ts, +layout.server.ts,
#     +server.ts or hooks.server.ts. SvelteKit fails the build if a $lib/server
#     module or $env/*/private is imported into client code.
#   - Never prefix a secret with PUBLIC_. That prefix marks a value as
#     browser-exposed and SvelteKit will happily ship it in the client bundle.
#   - Prefer `platform.env` or `$env/dynamic/private` over `$env/static/private`.
#     Static values are inlined at build time, and CI builds without production
#     secrets present, so a static read compiles to undefined in the deployed Worker.
#
# For staging and production, do NOT put values in a file. Push them to Cloudflare:
#   pnpm exec wrangler secret put NAME --env staging
#   pnpm exec wrangler secret put NAME --env production
